there's no blackboard or chalk or  notebooks  but there's a sparkle of hope in the  eyes of these young ethiopian refugees  trying to start a new life amid the  toughest of conditions  the makeshift school at the umra cuba  camp is sponsored by the norwegian  refugees council  one of the things we're trying to do is  to help give them a  sense of normalcy a bit of sense of  stability  by setting up some temporary learning  spaces  so they can have some classes every day  something to do but also something to to  learn from in this really chaotic period  of their life  so far we've set up 10 classes there'll  be  two shifts today we hope that that is  about 800  children that we can help in the first  instance  there's probably going to be a few  thousands children already  there's an estimated 3 000 children in  this camp  many of them had their education  disrupted when fighting broke out  between the ethiopian federal army  and their tigran people's liberation  front a few weeks ago  they were forced to flee with their  families relief agencies speak of a  number of unaccompanied children  in need of special care some are showing  signs of trauma  others are barely coping with the sudden  change in their lives  some have had it even worse the letter  sultan hasn't been able to find any  trace of his wife and two young  daughters  since the first raids on the tigran town  of hamera  by the sudanese border a few weeks ago  i just couldn't find them they could be  dead or alive but there's no idea what  happened to them  i was not home when the attack took  place people fled in every direction  they could  my family is gone we're still at the  umrakuba camp  and yes suddenly it looks like an old  place for some of the old ethiopians  here it's history repeating itself  that's because back in the 1980s this  building served as a school  for some of them as child refugees  in the 1980s refugees came here fleeing  a famine that coincided with  and resulted from years of fighting  between tigray rebels  and the central government of former  communist dictator mengistuhila mary  the animosity that conflict had created  still lingers today  here in the camp tigranes are teaching  their children  in english not amharic which is the main  official language of the federal  republic of ethiopia  a sign of the mammoth task ahead for  prime minister abi ahmed in his effort  to restore  national unity
 

